The fix is complete and compiles. Here's what I did:

## Change

**`crates/burn-jit/src/ops/int_ops.rs`** — the burn-jit backend was casting both operands to `u32` before the shift and leaving the result as `u32`, silently discarding the tensor's native signed element type `I` (i16/i32/i64) and truncating 64-bit values to 32 bits:

```rust
// before
fn bitwise_left_shift(lhs, rhs) -> IntTensor<Self> {
    let lhs_cast = kernel::cast::<R, I, u32>(lhs);
    let rhs_cast = kernel::cast::<R, I, u32>(rhs);
    launch_binop_int::<R, u32, kernel::BitwiseShlOp>(lhs_cast, rhs_cast)
}
```

```rust
// after — operates in the native element type I, matching bitwise_and/or/xor
fn bitwise_left_shift(lhs, rhs) -> IntTensor<Self> {
    launch_binop_int::<R, I, kernel::BitwiseShlOp>(lhs, rhs)
}

fn bitwise_left_shift_scalar(lhs, rhs: IntElem<Self>) -> IntTensor<Self> {
    launch_scalar_binop_int::<R, I, BitwiseShlOp>(lhs, rhs)
}
```

The `BitwiseShlOp` kernel is generic over `N: Int`, so it works directly with the native type without any cast — the declared element type is now preserved throughout.

## Why this is the right target

I surveyed all backends. Only two cast operands: burn-jit (to `u32`, and never restores the signed type — the buggy one) and burn-ndarray (to `i64` then narrows back to `I`). burn-jit is the backend that exercises i16/i32/i64/u32 int element types, so casting operands to `u32` corrupted i64 results and the signed element type. tch delegates to native libtorch (no cast), candle is `unimplemented!`, and fusion/router/autodiff just delegate.

I left the right-shift variants untouched (the task scoped this to left shift) and did **not** modify anything under `tests/` — the test's overflow-skip patch will be applied by the verification system.

Verified with `cargo check -p burn-jit` — clean.
